====Case====
Kharia Case-Syntagma has three cases:
* [[Nominative case|Nominative]]: unmarked
* [[Oblique case|Oblique]]: ''=te''
* [[Genitive case|Genitive]]: ''=aʔ''

The oblique case marker ''=te'' denotes a distinction between core and peripheral arguments. Participants marked with ''=te'' are interpreted as structurally and semantically external, in contrast to the nominative unmarked arguments. Thus, a Case-Syntagma marked with an oblique case marker is effectively blocked from forming part of the content head of a TAM/Person-Syntagma.<ref>Peterson (2011:110)</ref><ref>Peterson (2013:139)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|sahar{{=}}te{{=}}ki{{=}}ɲ|city{{=}}OBL{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}1SG|*Not applicable}}

The genitive marker =aʔ in Kharia does not mark the relationship of a Case-Syntagma to other syntagmas, such as a TAM/Person-Syntagma or another Case-Syntagma. Instead, it signals that the marked semantic base may function as part of a larger semantic head or that a certain postposition selects a semantic head in the genitive.<ref>Peterson (2013:154)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|oʔ{{=}}yaʔ teloŋ|house{{=}}GEN roof|'the roof of the house'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|og{{=}}aʔ bahare|house{{=}}GEN outside|'outside of the house'}}

Usually, the two cases do not co-occur on the same Case-Syntagma. However, it is possible for both to appear in the same Case-Syntagma for certain restricted contexts. Peterson notes that when the lexical head is not overtly mentioned due to its identity is already recoverable from the context or is considered unimportant, the oblique ''=te'' will attach to it, regardless of its grammatical status, which in some cases is a genitive determiner. This happens in two constructions: predicative alienable possession and locative meaning 'at the home/place of'.<ref>Peterson (2011:148)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=QUAL:qualitative predication|iɲ{{=}}aʔ{{=}}te saykal ayiˀj|me{{=}}GEN{{=}}OBL bicycle QUAL.PRES|'I have a bicycle.'}}

