The Sadri derived numerals often go with [[numeral classifiers]]. Classifiers occur very seldom with native numerals, at least by modern speakers, perhaps due to the unfamiliarity of the modern speakers with the Kharia numerals.<ref>Peterson (2011:188-189)</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Kharia classifiers
! Classifier
! Note
|-
|''cepuʔ''
|'handful'<ref>Peterson (2011:194)</ref>
|-
|''gaɽ''
|possibly derived from kaɽ 'human'<ref>Peterson (2011:194-195)</ref>
|-
|''go''
|Not attested by Peterson<ref>Peterson (2011:195)</ref>
|-
|''jan/jhan''
|used with human entities<ref>Peterson (2011:195-196)</ref>
|-
|''mene''
|multifunctional
|-
|''=o''
|general classifier, clitic<ref name="Peterson2011:198">Peterson (2011:198)</ref>
|-
|''ʈaŋ/ʈhaŋ/ʈhoŋ''
|similar to ʈho<ref>Peterson (2011:199)</ref>
|-
|''ʈho''
|generally non-human entities<ref>Peterson (2011:199-200)</ref>
|}

Except ''mene'' and ''gaɽ'', the mentioned classifiers of Kharia are well in line with the classifier systems of neighboring [[Eastern Indo-Aryan languages]] (notably Sardi), with cognate forms suggesting they were borrowed from Indo-Aryan. The use of classifiers in Kharia is optional, however. They often stand with cardinal numerals, and rarely, with quantifiers.<ref>Peterson (2011:194)</ref>

For the case of ''mene'', Peterson describes: "mene is the most elusive of the classifiers, and in fact, its status as a classifier is questionable." ''Mene'' can occur with not only different quantifiers but also any type of lexical heads.<ref>Peterson (2011:196)</ref> It may be found occurring with demonstratives, a genitive attributive, or even an adverbial.<ref>Peterson (2011:197)</ref> While its true function is still unknown, Peterson tentatively considers it a classifier.<ref name="Peterson2011:198"/>

====Modifiers ("Adjectives")====
From a morphosyntactic perspective, any contentive morpheme can function attributively to express the change of state (with middle voice) or a transitive telic action (with active voice). Thus, there is no evidence to assume a separate lexical class of "adjectives" in Kharia.<ref name=Peterson201>Peterson (2011:201)</ref> 

In attributive role, modifiers ("adjective") usually take the shapes of masdars or polysyllabic contentive morphemes. Due to [[bimoraic constraint]], monosyllabic contentive morphemes are required to form masdars in order to participate in attributive functions. The process is purely phonologically motivated and does not derived new stable lexical classes. The modifier is simply juxtaposed before the contentive morpheme that it modifies.<ref>Peterson (2011:200)</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
! Contentive morpheme !! Masdar !! Example
|-
| saŋ 'become yellow' || saŋ-saŋ || 
|-
| ruʔ 'open' || ruʔ-ruʔ || ruʔ-ruʔ kaˀbʈo 'open door'
|-
| rusuŋ 'red' || rusuŋ || rusuŋ oʔ 'red house'
|-
| maha 'large' || maha || maha daru 'big tree'
|-
| konon 'become small' || konon || konon bhai 'little brother'
|-
| ɖoko 'sit down; settle' || ɖoko || ɖoko ʈhãɽo 'settled place'
|-
| botoŋ 'become afraid' || botoŋ || botoŋ lebu 'frightened person'
|}

On the other hand, there are few monosyllabic contentive morphemes of mostly Indo-Aryan origin never reduplicate with an exception of col 'to go'. E.g. bes '(become) good', bes dinu 'good day'.<ref name=Peterson201/>

===TAM/Person-Syntagmas ("Verbs")===
Kharia lacks lexical categories of nouns and verbs, so any semantic bases/contentive morphemes, and even a whole complex Case-Syntagma structure corresponding to the ordinary "noun phrase" can function as the head in the predicative role within a syntactic phrase. In assembly with other functional morphemes, they form a TAM/Person-Syntagma, the predicative syntactic unit of a sentence. There are two major types of predicates in Kharia: narrative predication, which may resemble the typical predication in most languages, and equational predication (approximately "be" construction). The latter subtype consists of non-inherent qualitative predication and qualitative predication, both of which share the same structure as the narrative predicates except in the present tense. The qualitative predicative marker may also not be present, or "lacking", resulting in what resembles a "nominal sentence" but with subtle semantic differences to examples in which an element translating as 'be' or 'become' is present.<ref>Peterson (2011:209)</ref>

The simplified maximal structure of a non-periphrastic, fully finite narrative TAM/Person-Syntagma in Kharia:<ref>Peterson (2011:210)</ref>

{{interlinear
| indent = 2 |abbreviations=VOICE:active/middle voice; PERS:person
| top =  
|
|
|RECP CAUS-'''Lexeme'''/CAUS/ v2{{=}}PERF{{=}}TAM/VOICE{{=}}PERS/NUM/HON| style3 = font-variant: small-caps;
|
}}

The maximal structure of a fully finite, non-negative, non-periphrastic TAM/Person-Syntagma can be described as follows:<ref>Peterson (2011:211)</ref>

{{interlinear
| indent = 2 |abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb; VOICE:active/middle voice; AUTOPOES:autopoesis; EXCESS:excessive; CONT:continuative; SITER:semel-itemative; TEL:telicity; PERS:person
| top =  
|
|
|RECP CAUS-'''Lexeme'''/CAUS/ EXCESS BEN CONT/ITER/SITER PASS/REFL AUTOPOES TEL{{=}}PERF{{=}}TAM/VOICE{{=}}PERS/NUM/HON| style3 = font-variant: small-caps;
|
}}

That is, the content head of the syntagma can be a contentive morpheme or a complex Case-Syntagma structure, potentially modified by a causative prefix or infix.<ref>Peterson (2011:212)</ref> Following and supporting the semantic head is the functional head, containing a series of [[Light verb|explicator verbs]] or "v2", although their status as phonological words is somewhat unclear with respect to intonation, and many of them do not seem to carry any lexical meanings other than to mark voice, aspects, [[aktionsart]], and orientation to the TAM/Person-Syntagma. Functional morphemes such as TAM and Person/Number/Honorific are marked by enclitics attached to the rightmost element of the function head.<ref>Peterson (2011:293)</ref>

====Types of Semantic bases in TAM/Person-Syntagmas====
The most common type of semantic head of a finite TAM/Person Syntagma is a simple, non-derived contentive morpheme:

{{Interlinear|indent=3|gam{{=}}te{{=}}ki|talk{{=}}ACT.PRES{{=}}3PL|'They say'}}

A monosyllabic contentive morpheme can reduplicate to derive a masdar. The masdar TAM/Person-syntagma is used to denote various tenuous forms of TAM-aktionsart, such as remote past, habitual, or an event that took longer than usual. Masdars can only take the middle TAM markers.<ref>Peterson (2011:215)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=RDPL:reduplication|lam~lam{{=}}ki|RDPL~search{{=}}MID.PST|'S/he looked for [it] for a long time.' (Accepted in Simdega Kharia, rejected in Gumla Kharia)}}

A profound problem with the active-middle opposition that is fused in TAM categories is that it does not simply represent a distinction between transitive and intransitive predicates, nor do they restrictively function in transitivity marking. Peterson notes that the distribution of active-middle contrasts may also be indicated semantically. For example, the following contentive morphemes never occur in active predicates, since their semantics are inherently middle, which do not signify whether an additional semantic participant is involved, and the action is avolitional or eventive.

* ''aw'' 'live, stay'
* ''goˀj'' 'die'
* ''ajoˀɖ'' 'dry up (in a well)'
* ''gur'' 'fall'
* ''hoy'' 'become'

While many contentive morphemes are compatible with both the active and the middle voice with a contrast in interpretation, which Peterson describes as '''different functions''', some contentive morphemes of a particular semantic categories can only are restricted to only one voice depending on their prototypical meanings, which Peterson calls '''inherent functions'''.<ref>Peterson (2011:259)</ref>

Peterson identifies several semantic tendencies in contentive morphemes that permits ''different functions''. The middle is associated with intransitivity, spontaneous changes of state, durative processes and reduced volitionality. The active is associated with transitivity, externally directed action and potential volitional control. They are not all equally productive: some are widespread, whereas others are restricted to a small number of contentives or vary between speakers.<ref>Peterson (2011:276)</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Differential functions of Middle and Active
! Tendency !! Middle !! Active
|-
| Transitivity || Intransitive || Transitive
|-
| Aspectual || GENERIC = atelic, habitual, long duration, remote past, remote, indefinite future || Unmarked, telic
|-
| Direction || Self-directed || Outwardly directed
|-
| Thoroughness || Non-thorough || Thorough
|-
| Volitionary || Avolitional || Volitional
|-
| Punctuality || Non-punctual || Punctual
|-
| Action || (No action, SG)/(Long duration, DU/PL) ||  (action, SG)/(Shorter duration, DU/PL)
|-
| Concerning object || (Non-individuated object) || (Individuated object)
|-
| Stage of achievement || (Thwarted attempt) || (Successful action)
|-
| Participation || (Indirect participation) || (Direct participation)
|}

A significant number of Kharia contentive morphemes that might have originated from Sadri/Indo-Aryan employ augmentation to distinguish the middle and active contrasts.<ref>Peterson (2011:260)</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Contentive morphemes with different forms in Middle and Active
! Middle !! Meaning !! Active !! Meaning 
|-
| bagɽe || 'spoil' (INTR) || bagɽay || 'spoil' (TR), 'ruin'
|-
| cale || 'function', 'go' || calay || 'drive' (a car, etc.)
|-
| chuʔʈe || 'leave' (INTR) || chuʔʈay || 'leave' (TR)
|-
| khole || 'open' (INTR) || kholay || 'open' (TR)
|-
| ʈhane || 'try unsuccessfully to assert'  || ʈhanay || 'assert'
|}

Consequently, the lexical distribution of contentive morphemes that permit only one semantically assigned function (i.e., ''inherent function''), whether middle-only or active-only, such as those shown above (aw, goˀj, ajoˀɖ, gur, hoy,...etc) also exhibit a semantic distinction between middle and active, which is typically driven by similar tendencies such as transitivity, volitionary, and eventiveness.<ref>Peterson (2011:286)</ref> Inherently intransitive contentive morphemes that describe body-actions can be active-only if they are potentially controllable, such as ''khuʔ'' 'cough', ''hãʔchiŋ'' 'sneeze', ''iʔchõy'' ('fart').<ref>Peterson (2011:284)</ref> The table below summarizes the inherent functions:

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Inherent functions of the Middle and Active
! Tendency !! Middle !! Active
|-
| Transitivity || Intransitive || Transitive
|-
| Aspectual || Inherently long duration/Change of state || Unmarked
|-
| Volitionary || Avolitional || Potentially volitional
|}

====Subject marking====
Similar to [[Bonda language|Remo]], [[Gutob language|Gutob]], [[Gta' language|Gtaʔ]], and recently [[Juang language|Juang]], Kharia predicate only marks person/number of the subject argument.<ref>Peterson (2011:212)</ref> Distinction between animate and inanimate agents is not so profound in Kharia as they are both marked, although Biligiri (1965) stated that "there is a stronger tendency to observe number agreement with an animate subject than with an inanimate subject."<ref>Peterson (2008:500)</ref>
{| class="wikitable"
|-
! colspan="2" |
! singular
! dual (HON)
! plural
|-
! rowspan="2" | 1st person
! exclusive
| rowspan="2" | ''=ɲ(iɲ)/=ŋ(iŋ)''
| ''=jar''
| ''=le''
|-
! inclusive
| ''=naŋ''
| ''=niŋ''
|-
! colspan="2" | 2nd person
| ''=(e)m''
| ''=bar''
| ''=pe''
|-
! colspan="2" | 3rd person
| ''=Ø''
| ''=kiyar''
| ''=ki/=may''
|-
|}

====Tense, aspect, mood====
Kharia, like many Munda languages, merges TAM categories with active and middle voices.<ref>Peterson (2011:239-240)</ref>
{| class="wikitable"
|-
! 
! Middle
! Active
|-
! Present
| ''=ta''
| ''=te''
|-
! Present Progressive
| ''=taˀjɖ''
| ''=teˀjɖ''
|-
! Past Neutral (Past I)
| ''=ki''
| ''=(y)oʔ''
|-
! Irrealis
| ''=na''
| ''=e''
|-
! Past II
|colspan="2" | ''=khoʔ''
|-
! Perfect
|colspan="2" | ''=siʔ(ɖ)''
|-
! Optative
|colspan="2" | ''guɽuʔ/guɖuʔ''
|}

====Causative====
The causative derivation increases the valency of a contentive morpheme by introducing a higher or superordinate agent who causes the lower agent to act or a non-agentive event to happen. In Kharia, the signature marker of the Austroasiatic family ''-(o)(ʔ)b-'' (including allomorphs) is used as the causative prefix or infix.<ref>Peterson (2011:230-231)</ref> Double causative constructions are also allowed.<ref>Peterson (2011:233)</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
!root
!gloss
!Simple causative
!meaning
!Double causative
!meaning
|-
|aloŋ
|'sing'
|a-ˀb-loŋ
|'have someone sing'
|ob-a-ˀb-loŋ
|'someone make someone sing'
|-
|ɖeˀb
|'rise, climb'
|o-ɖeˀb
|'raise, offer up, sacrifice'
|oˀb-ɖeˀb
|'have someone sacrifice'
|-
|lemeˀɖ
|'go to bed'
|le-ʔ-meˀɖ
|'put someone to bed'
|oˀb-le-ʔ-meˀɖ
|'have someone put someone to bed'
|-
|sore
|'become ready'
|so-ˀb-re
|'prepare'
|ob-so-ˀb-re
|'have someone prepare'
|}

====Passive====
The [[passive voice]]/reflexive in Kharia is realized as a standalone word ''ɖom'', itself has no lexical meaning.<ref>Peterson (2011:293)</ref> Historically, it might have stemmed from the contentive morpheme ''dʒom'' ('to eat'), as it appears to cognate with [[Santali language|Santali]] passive ''-jɔn'' (< ''jɔm'' 'to eat') and [[Sora language|Sora]]-[[Juray language|Juray]] reflexive/low transitive denoting marker ''-dəm-''.<ref>Peterson (2011:297-298)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|pothi{{=}}ki ter '''ɖom'''{{=}}ki{{=}}may|book{{=}}PL give '''PASS'''{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'(The) Books '''were given.''''}}

====Reciprocal====
The Munda [[Reciprocal construction|reciprocal]] *kƏl/*qəl is manifested in Kharia as a free-standing word ''kol'', which has no lexical meaning.<ref name="Peterson2011:237"/> Whereas in other South Munda languages the reflexes of *kƏl exist as derivational prefixes and are used to derive new lexical verbs, the reciprocal marker in Kharia does not morphologically derive new words and does not directly change the meaning of the contentive morpheme of a TAM/Person-syntagma. In many cases, the interpretation of ''kol'' is broadly reciprocal, as illustrated: 

{{Interlinear|indent=3|ho{{=}}kiyar pothi{{=}}te '''kol''' lusu{{=}}ki{{=}}kiyar|that{{=}}DU book{{=}}OBL '''RECP''' grab{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}DU|'They grabbed '''each other''' book.'}}

In other contexts, it denotes what [[Frantisek Lichtenberk|Lichtenberk]] (1985) described as collective, i.e. two participants did something together.<ref name="Peterson2011:237">Peterson (2011:237)</ref> 

{{Interlinear|indent=3|'''kol''' aloŋ{{=}}ki{{=}}kiyar|RECP sing{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}DU|'They sang '''together'''.'}}

Lichtenberk also mentioned the third interpretation of the reciprocal is situation chaining,<ref>Peterson (2011:238)</ref> exemplified by the following sentence:

{{Interlinear|indent=3|'''kol''' buliˀj{{=}}taˀj{{=}}kiyar|'''RECP''' follow{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}DU|'They followed '''each other'''.'}}

====V2s ("Explicator verbs")====
=====Telicity=====
There are two telic markers in Kharia which serve the narrative structure:<ref>Peterson (2011:302)</ref>
* ''ɖoˀɖ'' (allomorph ɖoɽ) indicates that another event follows directly upon the event denoted by the predicate that it marks;

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=ATEL:anticipatory telic; QUAL:qualitative predication|Tay raja jhaɽi mudh kalo{{=}}ki{{=}}te ro yahudi jait{{=}}aʔ etoŋɖag{{=}}aʔ guru{{=}}ki{{=}}te ɖokloʔ remaʔ '''ɖoʈh'''{{=}}oʔ ro ho{{=}}ki{{=}}te "masih{{=}}te ate jorme{{=}}na ayiˀj?" gam{{=}}oʔ ro juŋ{{=}}oʔ|then king all chief priest{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL and Jew ethnic.group{{=}}GEN receiving.orders{{=}}GEN teacher{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL meeting call '''ATEL'''{{=}}ACT.PST and that{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL messiah{{=}}OBL where be.born{{=}}INF QUAL.PRES say{{=}}ACT.PST and ask{{=}}ACT.PST|'The king called all the chief priests and teachers of the Jewish people who received orders [from the king] to a meeting and asked (said and asked) them "Where is the Messiah to be born?"'}}

* ''goˀɖ'' (allomorph goɽ) denotes a turning point or culmination in a narrative.<ref>Peterson (2011:303)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CTEL:culminatory telic|saitan{{=}}aʔ fanda{{=}}te bajhe no '''goˀɖ'''{{=}}ta{{=}}iɲ|Satan{{=}}GEN net{{=}}OBL get.trapped Q '''CTEL'''{{=}}MID.PRES{{=}}1SG|'Will I get trapped in Satan's net?'}}

=====Durativity: continuative, semel-iterative and iterative=====
In situations in which an action or event persists for a longer period of time than expected, or keeps on in action while performing another, the continuative marker ''kan'' is used.<ref>Peterson (2011:299)</ref> 

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CONT:continuative; ADD:additive focus|ho{{=}}kaɽ kayom{{=}}ta ro kinbhar{{=}}jo joʔ '''kan'''{{=}}te|that{{=}}SG.HUM talk{{=}}MID.PRES and courtyard{{=}}ADD sweep '''CONT'''{{=}}ACT.PRES|'She talks and '''keeps sweeping''' the courtyard.'}}

With certain contentive morphemes, kan indicates that an action had been going on for a long time in the past before another action happened. With contentive morphemes denoting a volitional and motion event such as ''ɖoˀɖ'' 'take (away)', kan may express that the change is more-or-less permanent.

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CONT:continuative|iɲ am{{=}}bar{{=}}aʔ pothi{{=}}te ɖoˀɖ '''kan'''{{=}}oˀj|I 2{{=}}2.HON{{=}}GEN book{{=}}OBL take '''CONT'''{{=}}ACT.PST.1SG|'I took your book (and I'm not giving it back!).'}}

Semel-iterative, marked by ''loʔ'', denotes an event that occurs one additional time or is repeated once. It is used primarily by elder speakers with masdars.<ref>Peterson (2011:300)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SITER:semel-iterative|beroˀɖ '''loʔ'''{{=}}ki{{=}}may ro ɖoko loʔ{{=}}ki{{=}}may|stand.up '''SITER'''{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL and sit.down SITER{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'They kept on '''standing up and sitting down again''' (e.g., at a meeting).'}}

With some contentive morphemes, loʔ does not seem to mark iterativity, but rather a prolonged event, making its meaning very similar if not the same to that of continuative kan:<ref>Peterson (2011:301)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=RDPL:reduplication|gam~gam{{=}}'''loʔ'''{{=}}ki{{=}}may|RDPL~talk{{=}}'''CONT'''{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'They kept in talking'}}

The main marker of iterativity in Kharia is ''khor''. It indicates an action or event that comes to a stop and begins again, and may repeat in successive cycles.<ref>Peterson (2011:301–302)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=ITER:iterative; SEQ:sequential converb; RDPL:reduplication|ɖubhni{{=}}te peʔ yo{{=}}kon kowaʔ han{{=}}tiˀj u{{=}}tiˀj leŋ-leŋ '''khor'''{{=}}ki muda soloʔ{{=}}te yo{{=}}kon peʔ ɲoʔ{{=}}na um are{{=}}ki|cup{{=}}OBL rice see{{=}}SEQ crow that{{=}}side this{{=}}side fly-RDPL '''ITER'''{{=}}MID.PST but dog{{=}}OBL see{{=}}SEQ rice eat{{=}}INF NEG descend{{=}}MID.PST|'Having seen rice in the cup, the crow '''kept flying back and forth''', but having seen the dog, it didn't come down to feed.'}}

=====Ambulative=====
The ambulative in Kharia is ''san'' (< ''sangoɖ'' 'walk', cf. [[Santali language|Santali]] ''sɛn'' 'walk'), itself is no longer an independent lexical word. It denotes the action is carried out during movement from one place to another. 

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=AMB:ambulative; RDPL:reduplication|ol{{=}}na olna kayar beloŋ '''san'''{{=}}ki|bring{{=}}INF RDPL mango ripen '''AMB'''{{=}}MID.PST|'While being brought [to market], the mangoes ripened '''along the way'''.'}}

When san is used as v2 for four contentive morphemes ''ol'' 'bring', ''ɖoˀɖ'' 'take', ''col'' 'go', and ''ɖel'' 'come', it rather denotes the sense of 'along with' instead of ambulativity. Kullu suggests that san might be compatible with the aforementioned four contentive morphemes, and in fact the first example is rejected by some speakers as ungrammatical.<ref>Peterson (2011:306)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=AMB:ambulative; RDPL:reduplication|baysaki tay ...ho{{=}}ki moɲ baysaki koŋtaŋ ro baysali sãɽ ol '''san'''{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki|Baisali ABL that{{=}}PL one Baisali cow and Baisali ox bring '''AMB'''{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}3PL|'From Baisali. they brought one Baisali cow and Baisali ox '''along with them'''.'}}

=====Totality=====
Totality, expressed by ''may'', indicates completion or exhaustiveness of an action with respect to a particular participant of a scenario.<ref>Peterson (2011:304–306)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=TOTAL:totality; RDPL:reduplication|sori sori chaha uˀd '''may'''{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki (*ro tam jou uˀd{{=}}teˀj{{=}}ki)|together RDPL tea drink '''TOTAL'''{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL and now up.to drink{{=}}ACT.PROG{{=}}PL|'They drank up '''all''' the tea together (*and are still drinking).'}}

=====Suddenity=====
Three markers ''bhaʔ'', ''hambaʔ'', and ''dhaˀb'' are used as v2 in TAM/Person-Syntagmas to denote an event or action that occurs suddenly or quickly. None of these words carry any lexical meanings.<ref>Peterson (2011:306–307)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SUD:suddenness; RDPL:reduplication|ho{{=}}ki turthe{{=}}ga apan apan oɽiyay '''dhaˀb'''{{=}}na suru{{=}}yoʔ{{=}}ki|that{{=}}PL fast{{=}}FOC REFL RDPL put.in.order '''SUD'''{{=}}INF begin{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'They '''quickly''' began to put themselves in order.'}}

=====Departive=====
The departive marker ''ʈu'' denotes a situation in which the subject performed some action and then left. This word, again, carries no lexical meaning.<ref>Peterson (2011:307–308)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=DPT:departive|maha kulam paʔʈopur tay seŋ muʔ{{=}}ki{{=}}may. ho{{=}}ki kuɖaˀb aw{{=}}ki{{=}}may ho{{=}}ki{{=}}te u gam '''ʈu'''{{=}}yoʔ{{=}}ki|big brother Patna ABL first go.out{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL that{{=}}PL behind stay{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL that{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL this say '''DPT'''{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'The elder brothers set out from Patna first. They said to those who were behind (before they left):"..."'}}

=====Conative=====
When an action is attempted by a participant without being succeeded or completed, the TAM/Person-Syntagma may employ ''dakha'' (less frequently ''lakha'') to encode that type of modal-aktionsart. This word has no lexical meaning other than marking conativity to the predicate.<ref>Peterson (2011:308)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CONAT:conative; CNTR:contrastive focus|aɖ{{=}}ki{{=}}ko mane ɖel{{=}}ki{{=}}may arjowar gam '''dakha'''{{=}}yoʔ{{=}}ki|ANAPH{{=}}PL{{=}}CNTR umh come{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL Aryavarta say '''CONAT'''{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'They [i.e. others], uhm, came. They '''tried to''' call (say) [India] Aryavarta.'}}

=====Excessive=====
The Kharia equivalent to the English adverbial constructions involving 'strongly', 'intensely', 'extremely' is ''bay'', itself homophonous and undoubtedly derived from the same contentive morpheme which means literally 'make, do'.<ref>Peterson (2011:308–309)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=EXCES:excessive|muˀjdaʔ feinga{{=}}te leˀj '''bay'''{{=}}oʔ, "u chini iɲ{{=}}aʔ bohin{{=}}na{{=}}ɲ{{=}}ki thuray{{=}}siʔ{{=}}may" |ant grasshopper{{=}}OBL curse '''EXCES'''{{=}}ACT.PST this sugar 1SG{{=}}GEN sister{{=}}1.POSS{{=}}1SG{{=}}PL gather{{=}}PERF{{=}}3PL|'The ant gave the grasshopper a '''bad''' scolding, "This sugar my sisters have gathered."'}}

=====Autopoesis=====
In avolitional predicates, autopoetic marker ''jom'' denotes an action that happened on its own for the subject's own benefit, use, or maintenance, with the subject acting upon or for themselves. It may subtly imply an impolite connotation (i.e., act by oneself with no given permission). This word, although historically derived from an earlier contentive morpheme meaning 'eat', has become grammaticalized for autopoesis marking and is no longer found as an independent contentive morpheme.<ref>Peterson (2011:309)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=AUTOPOES:autopoesis|iɲ koʈhri{{=}}te badli '''jom'''{{=}}oˀj|I room{{=}}OBL change '''AUTOPOES'''{{=}}ACT.PST.1SG|'I just changed the room around (for no particular reason).'}}

=====Benefactive=====
Benefactive ''kay'' is used to denote that an action or event is performed for the benefit of another (external) participant. It does not carry any independent lexical meaning other than contributing the notion of 'do X on someone's behalf.' Peterson notes that kay is homophonous with the contentive morpheme ''kay'' which means 'to lift', but he also states that it appears unlikely to be the source of the benefactive marker.<ref>Peterson (2011:309–310)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CNTR:contrastive focus|khawna{{=}}ko peʈhiya geinka ol '''kay'''{{=}}na laʔ{{=}}ki{{=}}kiyar|a.kind.of.sweet{{=}}CNTR market every bring '''BEN'''{{=}}INF IPFV{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3DU|'And [at] every market they would '''bring sweets for''' him.'}}

=====Seldomly attested v2s=====
Peterson notes that there are few more v2s that have limited distributions in the corpus:

''col'' 'go' marks some types of durativity of the event.<ref>Peterson (2011:310)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|u{{=}}ghay kam{{=}}ga hoy{{=}}ga col{{=}}ki{{=}}may|this{{=}}way less{{=}}FOC become{{=}}FOC v2:go{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'Thus, they became fewer and fewer [went becoming few].'}}

''oˀj'' 'take out'. This apparently telic marker is only attested in Pinnow's corpus.<ref>Peterson (2011:310–311)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|ro kaiko hajar memon{{=}}te nuh{{=}}te ray och{{=}}oʔ ro nawka bay{{=}}na etoŋ{{=}}oʔ|and several thousand year{{=}}OBL Noah{{=}}OBL chose v2:take.out{{=}}ACT.PST and ship make{{=}}INF order{{=}}ACT.PST|'and after (in) several thousand years He (i.e. God) chose Noah, and ordered [him] to build a ship.'}}

''ol'' 'take'. This even rarer telic marker was uttered only twice by a Southern dialect speaker:<ref>Peterson (2011:311)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CNTR:contrastive focus|laʔ ho{{=}}te khali khaɽiya bheir aw{{=}}ki{{=}}may oɖoʔ hoɖom jait{{=}}ko mane ho{{=}}ki kuday{{=}}ga ol{{=}}teˀj{{=}}ki|then that{{=}}OBL only Kharia much live{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL and other ethnic.group{{=}}CNTR umh that chase{{=}}FOC v2:bring{{=}}ACT.PROG{{=}}PL|'Then only many Kharia lived there and, umh, they are driving away the other ethnic groups.'}}

''pal'' 'finish'. This contentive morpheme however means "be able" when it functions as an auxiliary.<ref>Peterson (2011:311–312)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb|chiɽopoɽo koŋ laɖ{{=}}na mare{{=}}yoʔ{{=}}ki laɖ pal{{=}}ke cah sobre{{=}}yoʔ{{=}}ki|quickly bread bake{{=}}INF begin{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL bake v2:finish{{=}}SEQ tea prepare{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'they quickly began to bake bread. When they had finished baking, they prepared tea.'}}

''safay'' 'clean'. In both examples uttered by a Southern dialect speaker, it is found in context of expressing a negative action or feeling. Its true function is still undetermined due to scant data.<ref>Peterson (2011:312–313)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|poʔda{{=}}ʔ jhaɽi lebu{{=}}ki botoŋ safay{{=}}ki{{=}}may|village{{=}}GEN all person{{=}}PL fear v2:clean{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'All the people of the village were very afraid.'}}

====Partially-Finite and Non-Finite Predicates====
=====Partially-Finite Predicates=====
In certain restricted types of daily discourse, such as events consisting of two sub-events that are highly related, there would be a non-final sub-predicate lacking some functional markers, often the person/number marker. Those partly-marked TAM/Person-Syntagma construction denoting co-subordination of events, that the following highly-connected action/event is being done by the same argument. They also mark the hierarchy of finiteness at clausal level, with the most finite TAM/Person-Syntagma receives full marking. The two sub-events may be directly juxtaposed next to each other or may be joined by a conjunction like ''ro'' or ''oɖoʔ''.<ref>Peterson (2011:313–314)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|onɖor{{=}}e ro sikhe{{=}}na{{=}}niŋ|hear{{=}}ACT.IRR and learn{{=}}MID.IRR{{=}}1PL.INCL|'We should hear and learn [his teachings].'}}

=====Masdars=====
The unmarked bisyllabic and phonologically-motivated reduplicated form of a contentive morpheme or ''the masdar'' in Kharia can function in TAM/Person-Syntagmas as what is described as a "[[verbal noun]]" in Indo-European linguistics, i.e. a non-finite predicate that may also assume the referential and attributive functions.<ref>Peterson (2011:317–319)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=QUAL:qualitative predication; CMPL:complementizer; RDPL:reduplication|no prabhu{{=}}waʔ janam{{=}}te mane ho{{=}}ki{{=}}te iswar{{=}}aʔ '''daŋ'''-'''daŋ''' aw{{=}}ki,...|CMPL God{{=}}GEN birth{{=}}OBL umh that{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL Lord{{=}}GEN '''send'''-'''RDPL''' QUAL{{=}}MID.PST|'that at God's birth, umh, the Lord had sent to them [His Son] ([Jesus] was a '''sent-one''' of the Lord),...'}}

=====Infinitives=====
The [[infinitive]] marker in Kharia is ''=na'', which appears to have emerged from the same origin with the middle irrealis ''=na''. Peterson posits that the Kharia infinitive development may resemble what found in nearby [[Tibeto-Burman language|Tibeto-Burman]] [[Newari language|Newari]] in [[Nepal]] where a nonpast verbal form is also used to mark infinitive. The Kharia infinitive also seems to cognate with [[North Dravidian languages|North Dravidian]] [[Kurukh language|Kurukh]] infinitival form ''-na'' as well.<ref>Peterson (2011:322–324)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=QUAL:qualitative predication; RDPL:reduplication|muda dada{{=}}ɖom{{=}}ki{{=}}yaʔ aw-aw{{=}}te ber beʈi{{=}}ki{{=}}te um uɖuŋ{{=}}'''na''' pal{{=}}e|but elder.brother{{=}}3.POSS{{=}}PL{{=}}GEN QUAL-RDPL{{=}}OBL who daughter{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL NEG make.flee{{=}}'''INF''' be.able{{=}}ACT.IRR|'But as long as their elder brothers are present, no one will be able '''to drive''' the daughters away.')}}

=====Sequential converbs=====
There are three sequential converb ("conjunctive participles") markers in Kharia: ''=ker'', ''=ke'', and ''=kon''. The first two markers were borrowed directly from Sadri, and the last apparently derived from the contentive morpheme ''ikon'' ('make, do'), calqued from Indo-Aryan construction due to contact with Sadri and Hindi.<ref>Peterson (2011:326)</ref> These markers denote that sequential and temporal events being directly related and associated to one another in some way, combining to express a larger, complex event.<ref>Peterson (2011:324)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb; RDPL:reduplication|dhirom dhirom khaɽiya maha rokeˀd-loʔ paro{{=}}'''kon''' rusuŋ samuder ɖam{{=}}ki{{=}}may|slowly RDPL Kharia big sand-land cross{{=}}'''SEQ''' red ocean arrive{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'Slowly but surely, the Kharia '''crossed''' the great desert and arrived at the Red Sea.'}}

There is a tendency that speakers would use sequential converbs to clauses which feature the same subject/agent (S/A). Although it is clearly a trend among speakers and sequential converbs can appear in different subject clauses as well, the latter use is considered uncommon. In fact, some consulted speakers reject the use of sequential converbs with different subject contexts as ungrammatical.<ref>Peterson (2011:327–328)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb; CTEL:culminatory telic|thor toʔ aw{{=}}'''kon''' jiŋray{{=}}aʔ sori ɖaŋ goʈh{{=}}oʔ|few day stay{{=}}'''SEQ''' porcupine{{=}}GEN with send CTEL{{=}}ACT.PST|'[porcupine] '''having stayed''' a few days, [father] sent [daughter] off with the porcupine.'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb; EMOT:emotive predicate marker|etwa{{=}}te u{{=}}ki{{=}}yaʔ haleit yo{{=}}'''kon''' lebui laʔ{{=}}ki|Etwa{{=}}OBL this{{=}}PL{{=}}GEN condition see{{=}}'''SEQ''' love EMOT{{=}}MID.PST|'Etwa, '''seeing''' their condition, felt compassion.'}}

=====Imperfective converbs=====
Imperfective converbs (traditionally referred to as "present participles") in Kharia are used to indicate an event that happens simultaneously with main eventuality of the main finite predicate.<ref>Peterson (2011:330)</ref> There are four types of imperfective converbial constructions. The first three constructions employ marking on the semantic head of non-finite predicate and reduplication.<ref>Peterson (2011:331)</ref>

The genuine converb ''=ta'', which might have derived from the present middle ''=ta'', although the two are distinct in morphosyntactic behaviors. 

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CVB:imperfective converb; RDPL:reduplication|ho{{=}}ki fawɖ guˀɖ katiˀb{{=}}ki{{=}}may ro hathiyar ɖoˀɖ{{=}}'''ta''' '''ɖoˀɖta''' seŋgor-ɖaʔ raylogaɽh hinte caɽhai karay{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki|that{{=}}PL army like assemble{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL and weapon take{{=}}'''CVB''' '''RDPL''' straight-INT Railogarh LOC attack do{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'They assembled like an army, and, '''taking''' their weapons, [went] straight to Railogarh and there they attacked [the Kharia].'}}

Focus marker ''=ga'' can also functions as denoting semantic depictives:

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=AUTOPOES:autopoesis; RDPL:reduplication|ro ho kuda koloŋ{{=}}aʔ daru sumbhoʔ{{=}}te iɲam{{=}}'''ga''' '''iɲamga''' goj jom{{=}}ta|and that millet bread{{=}}GEN tree base{{=}}OBL cry{{=}}'''FOC''' '''RDPL''' die AUTOPOES{{=}}MID.PRES|'And '''crying and crying''', she just dies [through sorrow] at the base of that millet bread tree.'}}

The infinitive marker ''=na'' may occur in imperfective converbial constructions.

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=RDPL:reduplication; EMOT:emotive predicate marker|...lekin lam{{=}}'''na''' '''lamna''' souˀb{{=}}te ikuɖ jughay daʔ piyas laʔ{{=}}ki|but search{{=}}'''INF''' '''RDPL''' all{{=}}OBL very much water thirst EMOT{{=}}MID.PST|'...But '''searching and searching''', (they) all became very thirsty.'}}

Unmarked, repeated masdar and echo-formation of polysyllabic contentive morphemes can also play the role similar to that of imperfective converb markers since they denote the senses of durativity and iterativity, thus marking the secondary event as simultaneous within the time frame of the main event. Masdar and echo word imperfective converbs are restricted to polysyllabic morphemes.<ref>Peterson (2011:333)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb; RDPL:reduplication; ECHO:echo word|modi ro saw-ɽay{{=}}ɖom ayo aba ro boker kulam{{=}}ɖom{{=}}ki{{=}}yaʔ kaʈa sumboʔ{{=}}te gur{{=}}kon ĩyam '''ĩyam''' maf bor{{=}}oʔ{{=}}kiyar|Modi and spouse-woman{{=}}3.POSS mother father and brother.in.law brother{{=}}3.POSS{{=}}PL{{=}}GEN foot base{{=}}OBL fall{{=}}SEQ cry '''RDPL''' forgiveness ask{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}DU|'Modi and his wife fell down at the feet of his mother, father, brother-in-law and brothers, '''crying''', asked for forgiveness..'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=RDPL:reduplication; ECHO:echo word|ho{{=}}ki poʔda{{=}}ɖom{{=}}ki{{=}}yaʔ halet onɖor{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki, kunɖuʔ hakon{{=}}ɖom{{=}}ki{{=}}te sumtay{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki ro kuda '''kudi''' khaɽiya ɖay{{=}}ki{{=}}yaʔ kunɖaˀb kunɖaˀb yar{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki|that{{=}}PL village{{=}}3.POSS{{=}}PL{{=}}GEN condition hear{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL child ECHO{{=}}3.POSS{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL gather{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL and hurry '''ECHO''' Kharia woman{{=}}PL{{=}}GEN back RDPL flee{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'They heard what was happening in the villages(the condition of their villages), gathered their children and ''hurriedly'' fled after the Kharia women.'}}

=====Participles=====
Kharia has two unambiguously [[participle]] markers, ''-l'' and ''=wala'', both of which were borrowed from Sadri. The former only attaches to loan words of Sadri origin, e.g. biha 'marry', biha-l 'married'. It is restricted to past tense event only and has limited acceptance among speakers.<ref name="Peterson2011:334">Peterson (2011:334)</ref>

The second marker ''=wala'' is accompanied with infinitive ''=na''. It is used to express iterative or habitual senses.<ref>Peterson (2011:333)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=PTCP:participle|kamu{{=}}na{{=}}wala lebu{{=}}ki|work{{=}}INF{{=}}PTCP person{{=}}PL|'working people'}}

The third, mysterious and native ''-ɖuʔ'' can be somewhat accepted as a participle marker, although it may be encountered occurring with contentive morphemes as a derivational marker carrying the approximative meaning of "-ish". E.g. maha-ɖuʔ 'biggish'.<ref>Peterson (2011:69)</ref> When this suffix functions as participle marker, there is no temporal orientation and the participle may refer to past, present or future events.<ref name="Peterson2011:334"/>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=PTCP:participle|dilli co-ɖuʔ lebu|Delhi go-PTCP person|'the man, the person who went to Delhi'}}

====Negation====
The negative particles in Kharia cover the scope of TAM/Person syntagma, which is a syntactically clausal predicative structure. For primary predicates, there are two major types of negation, depending on the status of the TAM/Person-Syntagma. The table below summarizes types of negators found in Kharia.<ref name="Peterson2011:334"/>

{| class="wikitable"
! Negator
! Note
|-
|''um''
|General; non-irrealis and non-imperative optative
|-
|''abu''
|Imperative and optative
|-
|''umboʔ''
|Emphatic 
|}

There is also a fourth negative marker ''na'' which has been borrowed from Indo-Aryan, however, has a very seldom use. It typically occurs in either negative commands, with the infinitive, or in a ''neither...nor'' expression with a finite TAM/Person-Syntagma:<ref>Peterson (2011:341)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|Modi '''na''' siloʔ{{=}}na laʔ{{=}}ki no oɖoʔ{{=}}ga hoɖom kamu karay{{=}}na laʔ{{=}}ki|Modi '''neither''' plow{{=}}INF IPFV{{=}}MID.PST or more{{=}}FOC other work do{{=}}INF IPFV{{=}}MID.PST|'Modi '''neither''' plowed '''nor''' did any other work.'}}

=====General negative ''um'' =====
This negative marker always precedes the content head of now-partially finite TAM/Person-Syntagma. It pulls the subject marker away from its original attachment place, except the singular (partially), dual and plural second person markers.<ref>Peterson (2011:335)</ref>

Simplified schematic overview of a negated TAM/Person-Syntagma:

{{interlinear
| indent = 2 |abbreviations=VOICE:active/middle voice; PERS:person
| top =  
|
|
|NEG{{=}}PERS/NUM/HON RECP CAUS-'''Lexeme'''/CAUS/ v2{{=}}PERF{{=}}TAM/VOICE| style3 = font-variant: small-caps;
|
}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|'''umay''' juŋ{{=}}oʔ|'''NEG.3PL''' ask{{=}}ACT.PST|'They didn't ask'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|ubhroŋ '''um'''{{=}}em ɖe{{=}}na|these.days '''NEG'''{{=}}2SG come{{=}}MID.IRR|'These days you do not come.'}}

or  {{Interlinear|indent=3|ubhroŋ '''um''' ɖe{{=}}na{{=}}m|these.days '''NEG''' come{{=}}MID.IRR{{=}}2SG|'These days you do not come.'}}

''Um'' is also the negative contentive morpheme in general where it is placed before another contentive morpheme:<ref>Peterson (2011:336)</ref>

* Um lereʔ 'unhappy'; lereʔ 'joy, rejoice, joyous'
* Um suʔkhoʔ-boʔ 'uncomfortable'; sukhoʔ 'pleasure, happiness, happy' + -boʔ '{{gcl|INT}}'

=====Modal negator ''abu'' =====
This negative marker is often used with prohibitive or optative constructions, although ''um'' may occur in the irrealis if the situation is not considered imperative. Note it has three version marked for person/number: abu '{{gcl|2SG}}', aʔbar '{{gcl|2DU}}/{{gcl|HON}}', aʔpe '{{gcl|2PL}}'. The form abu can also be used to refer to a 3rd person argument.<ref>Peterson (2011:338)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CNTR:contrastive focus|muda soloʔ{{=}}te{{=}}ko lemeɖ{{=}}ga '''abu''' ɖe{{=}}na|but dog{{=}}OBL{{=}}CNTR sleep{{=}}FOC '''NEG.MOD''' come{{=}}MID.IRR|'But the dog just couldn't get any sleep' (but to the dog, sleep '''should not''' come).}}

=====Emphatic negative ''umboʔ'' =====
This negative marker is used in many situations, commonly in answers-to-questions to denote emphasis. It may occur at the end of clause after a finite TAM/Person-Syntagma, like the example below:<ref>Peterson (2011:339)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CNTR:contrastive focus|jiŋray diyo{{=}}ga laroʔ daru{{=}}te yo{{=}}na co{{=}}na laʔ{{=}}ki muda goj{{=}}ta '''umboʔ'''|porcupine daily{{=}}FOC date.palm tree{{=}}OBL see{{=}}INF go{{=}}INF IPFV{{=}}MID.PST but die{{=}}MID.PRES '''no'''|'The porcupine went to see the date-palm tree daily, but it '''just wouldn't''' die.}}

''Umboʔ'' can occur with masdars as well, particularly in clauses that features high contextual density, where it may denote the sense of 'could not.' E.g. umboʔ col-col 'I could not go'. Consulted speakers state that in such contexts, only the first person arguments (i.e. the speakers) could be interpreted as the primary argument that the negated clause refers to. However, this interpretation appears to be only tendentially, as example from Pinnow (1965) shows that it does not carry a restricted reference to the first person arguments.<ref>Peterson (2011:340–341)</ref>

====Periphrastic TAM/Person-Syntagmas ("Phase verbs")====
What listed below are few contentive morphemes that may appear as the finite functional head in a number of complex predicative structures. They act as periphrastic aspectual and modal encoders for lexical content head in infinitival forms. The resulted complex TAM/Person-Syntagmas resemble typical English auxiliary verb constructions.<ref>Peterson (2011:341)</ref>

''Imperfectivity laʔ''. Being the most common type of the periphrastic aspectual marker, it generally denotes the sense of imperfectivity, including iterativity, habituality and non-present progressivity. It seems to be derived from Indo-Aryan ''lag-'' (meaning "attach", though "begin" in other occasions when it follows an infinitive). It may also express with an inceptive meaning, which is fairly common among the languages in [[Jharkhand]] and [[Bengal]].<ref>Peterson (2011:342)</ref> 

In the following example, ''laʔ'' serves as introduction of the next scene background/new action of the story while simultaneously conveys imperfectivity.<ref>Peterson (2011:343)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb|hobne{{=}}te{{=}}ga ubar koleʔ kunɖuʔ jal{{=}}te bajhe{{=}}kon ʈãy ʈãy toroˀɖ{{=}}na '''laʔ'''{{=}}ki{{=}}kiyar|that.much{{=}}OBL{{=}}FOC two parrot child net{{=}}OBL get.trapped{{=}}SEQ tay tay cry{{=}}INF '''IPFV'''{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}DU|'Meanwhile (in that much), two baby parrots got caught in the net and '''began''' crying "Tay! Tay."'}}

''mãɽe, maɽe, suru'' 'begin'. Unlike ''laʔ'', these words only have this one meaning.<ref>Peterson (2011:345)</ref>
{{Interlinear|indent=3|jeʔ botoŋ buŋ thartharay{{=}}na '''mãɽe'''{{=}}yoʔ{{=}}ki|so fear INST tremble{{=}}INF '''begin'''{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'So (they) '''began''' to tremble with fear.'}}

''melay'' 'stop'
{{Interlinear|indent=3|soloʔ bhabru{{=}}na '''melay'''{{=}}oʔ|dog bark{{=}}INF '''stop'''{{=}}ACT.PST|'The dog '''stopped''' barking.'}}

''col'' 'go'
{{Interlinear|indent=3|tama iɲ aniɲ{{=}}aʔ gotar{{=}}aʔ kahni batay{{=}}na '''col'''{{=}}taˀjɖ{{=}}iɲ|now I We.INCL{{=}}GEN clan{{=}}GEN story tell{{=}}INF '''go'''{{=}}MID.PROG{{=}}1SG|'Now I am '''going''' to tell the story of our clan.'}}

''lam'' 'look for, want'. With animate subject, the marker denotes the volition of the subject who ''wants to do''. With inanimate subject,  it denotes that the action ''is imminent''. The latter interpretation has limited acceptance among speakers.<ref>Peterson (2011:347)</ref>
{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb|iɲ u ikuˀɖ sundar kontheˀɖ{{=}}ki{{=}}te bajhay{{=}}kon satay{{=}}na um{{=}}iɲ '''lam'''{{=}}te|I this very beautiful bird{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL trap{{=}}SEQ torment{{=}}INF NEG{{=}}1SG '''want'''{{=}}ACT.PRES|'I don't '''want''' to trap these beautiful birds and torment them.'}}

''ter'' 'give'. This contentive morpheme is found with infinitive content head in permissive constructions.
{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=CNTR:contrastive focus; QUAL:qualitative predication|umboʔ, jiyom aw{{=}}na bheir{{=}}ko ho{{=}}ghay{{=}}na um{{=}}niŋ '''ter'''{{=}}e|NEG life QUAL{{=}}INF up.to{{=}}CNTR that{{=}}way{{=}}INF NEG{{=}}1PL.INCL '''allow'''{{=}}ACT.IRR|'No! As long as there is life [in us], we will not '''allow''' that to happen. (we will not allow [it] to become that way).'}}

''pal'' 'be able' as a periphrastic TAM/person-syntagma. It however means 'to finish' when function as an explicator verb.<ref>Peterson (2011:348)</ref>
{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SUD:suddenness|paʔʈopur{{=}}te{{=}}ga ho{{=}}ki ikuɖ{{=}}ga kisro{{=}}sikh{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki hin{{=}}aʔ caʔɖom ho{{=}}ki rohtaspur hinte ɖoko dhab{{=}}na '''pal'''{{=}}oʔ{{=}}ki|Patna{{=}}OBL{{=}}FOC that{{=}}PL much{{=}}FOC wealthy{{=}}PERF{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL that{{=}}GEN for that{{=}}PL rohitasgarh LOC sit.down SUD{{=}}INF '''be.able'''{{=}}ACT.PST{{=}}PL|'In Patna they had become very wealthy, therefore they were '''able''' to settle down quickly in Rohitasgarh.'}}

====Inference====
The contentive morpheme ''hoy'' ('become') is used to express what are termed by Peterson (2000) as "inference through reasoning", in which no direct evidence is involved, as well as "inference through direct results" in certain contexts. It always occupied the sentence-final position, directly preceded by a TAM/Person-syntagma.<ref>Peterson (2011:349–350)</ref> In the following example, a speaker went out in the morning, saw that the ground was wet, and inferred that it must have rained last night:
{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=INFER:inference|ɖaʔ ɖel{{=}}ki '''hoy'''|water come{{=}}MID.PST '''INFER'''|'It must have rained' (water must have come, I guess).}}

====Qualitative Predication====
Since Kharia lacks structural evidence for the existence of lexical categories of nouns and verbs, the term "[[Copula (linguistics)|copula]]" and its relative concept of [[nominal sentence]] are not applicable in this language. These concepts are subsumed under qualitative predications, which are divided into two types: stative qualitative predication ('to be') and dynamic qualitative predication ('to become'). The presence of qualitative predicative markers denotes a "narrative description", while the absence of those indicates "a general or self-evident truth".<ref>Peterson (2011:376–377)</ref> 

In the ''stative qualitative predication'' category, there are three types of markers of stative qualitative predication: non-inherent, inherent, and neutral. The non-inherent markers express non-inherent, temporal, context-based, situational contingent qualities. The inherent markers express relatively inherent, defining properties to a referent. Both are limited to describe the current time only.<ref name="Peterson2011:378"/>

{| class="wikitable"
! Type of stative qualitative predication !! Non-negated form !! Negated form
|-
| Inherent qualities || ''heke'' || ''nalage''
|-
| Non-inherent qualities || ''ayiˀj(ɖ)'' || ''umboriˀj(ɖ)''
|}

''Inherent qualities'': {{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=QUAL:qualitative predication|ho{{=}}kaɽ hosiyar '''heke'''|that{{=}}SG.HUM intelligent '''QUAL.PRES'''|'She is '''the intelligent one'''.'}}

''Non-inherent qualities'': {{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=QUAL:qualitative predication|ho{{=}}kaɽ hosiyar '''ayiˀj'''|that{{=}}SG.HUM intelligent '''QUAL.PRES'''|'She is '''intelligent'''.'}}

Heke and nalage have been borrowed directly from [[Nagpuri language|Sadri]]. Malhotra (1982) cites some examples in which the negative form of heke is ''um heke'' instead of nalage. They are, however, quite rare, and nalage is considered standard.<ref>Peterson (2011:379)</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
|+Present-tense paradigm of non-negated, inherent qualitative marker
|-
! colspan="2" |
! singular
! dual (HON)
! plural
|-
! rowspan="2" | 1st person
! exclusive
| rowspan="2" | ''hek=iɲ''
| ''heke=jar''
| ''heke=le''
|-
! inclusive
| ''heke=naŋ''
| ''heke=niŋ''
|-
! colspan="2" | 2nd person
| ''hek=em''
| ''heke=bar''
| ''heke=pe''
|-
! colspan="2" | 3rd person
| ''heke''
| ''heke=kiyar''
| ''heke=ki/heke=may/hekãy''
|-
|}

On the other hand, ayiˀj(ɖ) appears to be an Indo-Aryan loan, but which language it originated from is not clearly known. Peterson suggests a broadly Eastern Indo-Aryan origin, citing a cognate with the [[Maithili language|Maithili]] 3rd person non-honorific proform ''əich'' 'he/she/it'.<ref>Peterson (2011:377)</ref>

{| class="wikitable"
|+Present-tense paradigm of non-negated, non-inherent qualitative marker
|-
! colspan="2" |
! singular
! dual (HON)
! plural
|-
! rowspan="2" | 1st person
! exclusive
| rowspan="2" | ''ayiˀjɖ=iɲ''
| ''ayiˀj=jar''
| ''ayiˀj=le''
|-
! inclusive
| ''ayiˀj=naŋ''
| ''ayiˀj=niŋ''
|-
! colspan="2" | 2nd person
| ''ayiˀjɖ=em''
| ''ayiˀj=bar''
| ''ayiˀj=pe''
|-
! colspan="2" | 3rd person
| ''ayiˀj''
| ''ayiˀj=kiyar''
| ''ayiˀj=ki/ayiˀj=may''
|-
|}

With the neutral stative qualitative predicative marker ''aw'' (derived from the same contentive morpheme meaning 'live, stay, remain'), the distinction between inherent and non-inherent qualities is disabled.<ref name="Peterson2011:378">Peterson (2011:378)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=QUAL:qualitative predication|jhaɽi poʔda hinte muɖu la muɖu gãysi '''aw'''{{=}}ta{{=}}ki{{=}}ga|all village LOC one.HUM then one.HUM spy '''QUAL'''{{=}}MID.PRES{{=}}PL{{=}}FOC|'In all villages there are [always] some spies.'}}

Kharia uses two contentive morphemes ''hoy'' and ''bone'', both were borrowed from Sadri, to express the ''dynamic qualitative predication'' category ('to become', or more accurately, 'to take place').<ref>Peterson (2011:381)</ref>

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=ADD:additive focus|u jharkhanɖ alag '''hoy'''{{=}}ki ho bhere tay ghaɖ, iskul koʔghel{{=}}jo ho{{=}}ki khaɽiya kayam{{=}}ta{{=}}may|this Jharkhand separate '''become'''{{=}}MID.PST that time ABL therefore school vinicity{{=}}ADD that{{=}}PL Kharia speak{{=}}MID.PRES{{=}}3PL|'Since the time Jharkhand '''became''' independent, therefore they speak Kharia in school as well.'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=QUAL:qualitative predication|ʈoʔpo aw{{=}}ki, adha fura niga '''bone'''{{=}}ki{{=}}may|toppo.bird QUAL{{=}}MID.PST half part Kurukh '''become'''{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'There was Toppo [a kind of bird], they [his desdendants, the Toppo clan] '''became''' half Kurukh.'}}

===Syntax===
====Syntax of the simple sentence====
The way of person marking in TAM/Person-Syntagmas and case-marking in Case-Syntagmas clearly shows that Kharia is a [[nominative-accusative]] language. The unmarked constituent order is Subject–Object–Predicate or predicate-final, although the primary arguments may not be explicitly mentioned and Case-Syntagmas may appear in different positions with respect to their pragmatic status, followed by various types of [[information structure]] elements depending on discourse-oriented factors. 

==Sample text==
{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb; CMPL:complementizer; ADD:additive focus|Ughay andai ɖom{{=}}ta no ata bhere israeli lebu{{=}}ki rusuŋ samudar{{=}}te paro{{=}}na laʔ{{=}}ki, hin bhere{{=}}jo khaɽiya{{=}}kia ho boʔ{{=}}ki{{=}}te{{=}}ga aw{{=}}ki{{=}}may.|this.way guess PASS{{=}}MID.PRES CMPL Q time Israeli person{{=}}PL red ocean{{=}}OBL cross{{=}}INF IPFV{{=}}MID.PST then time{{=}}ADD Kharia{{=}}PL that place{{=}}PL{{=}}OBL{{=}}FOC live{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL.SUBJ|'Thus it is assumed that at the time that the Israelis were crossing the Red Sea, at that time the Kharias were at those places as well.'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=SEQ:sequential converb; CMPL:complementizer|Laʔ anin{{=}}aʔ khariya bulbul, yane babilon, poʔda tay muʔ{{=}}kon del/em/-dol arloʔ, serloʔsin ghay hoy{{=}}kon utiˀj del{{=}}ki{{=}}may.|then 1PL.INCL{{=}}GEN Kharia Babylon i.e. Babylon village ABL emerge{{=}}SEQ come-? north south way become{{=}}SEQ this.side come{{=}}MID.PST{{=}}3PL|'Then our Kharia [ancestors], having left Bulbul, i.e., Babylon, coming
along, via the north and south, came to this side [i.e., here].'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|abbreviations=RDPL:reduplication; QUAL:qualitative predication|Am{{=}}ga patar terter heke{{=}}m, am, Yesu, ɖe{{=}}na{{=}}m ro patar ter{{=}}e{{=}}m patar ter{{=}}e{{=}}m.|You{{=}}FOC light give.RDPL QUAL.PRES{{=}}2SG You Jesus come{{=}}MID.IRR{{=}}2SG and light give{{=}}ACT.IRR{{=}}2SG light give{{=}}ACT.IRR{{=}}2SG|'You are the [one] who gives light, you, Jesus, will come and give light, you will give light.'}}

